}

\section{Overview}\label{Overview}

We want to take the times at which the four assignments in the datintro 
module (the assignment group LAB1) were passed and compute who finished 
first.
To finish first, they must have passed all four assignments.
We want to write a small shell script [[<<[[top.sh]]>>]] that prints the 
top 10 to standard output (stdout).
\ltnote{%
  We give the desired output and what the input looks like.
  This is the undivided whole.
  Later we divide the problem.
}%
The result should look like this:
\begin{pycode}
import subprocess

submissions_file = "submissions26.csv"
examples = "(Severholt|Bergengren|Wilmer)"

def shell(cmd):
  output = subprocess.run(cmd,
                          shell=True,
                          stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                          stdout=subprocess.PIPE)
  print(r"\begin{minted}[breaklines]{text}")
  print(output.stdout.decode().replace("\r\n", "\n").strip())
  print(r"\end{minted}")

shell(f"bash ./top.sh {submissions_file} 10")
\end{pycode}

We get the data containing all submissions by the line
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms submissions list -c "^datintro26$" -A LAB1 > submissions26.csv
\end{minted}
The option [[-c]] picks the course by a regular expression (regex).
The [[^]] and [[$]] say that the name must be exactly [[datintro26]], nothing 
more, so that we don't get [[datintro26p3]] as well if there is such a course.
The option [[-A]] picks the assignment group, in our case LAB1.
That way we get exactly the four assignments that we care about and, as we'll 
see, exactly four lines per student\footnote{%
  Without [[-A LAB1]] we'd get every assignment in the course.
  Most of the others are graded with points instead of P or F, so they'd never 
  count as passed, but their lines would still be in the way when we count 
  how many assignments a student has passed.
}.
The data is in CSV format.
It looks something like this:
\begin{pycode}
shell(f"egrep '{examples}' {submissions_file} | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}
Each column, or field, is separated by a tab.
(Here we replaced the tabs by two spaces to make them visible, so the space 
inside a time stamp is not a column separator.)
The columns are: the course, the assignment, the student, the grade, the 
submission date and the grade date.
The value [[N/A]] means that there is no value.
These four assignments aren't handed in through Canvas, the grade is entered 
by a teaching assistant, so the submission date is always [[N/A]].
It's the grade date, the last column, that tells us when a student passed.
Note that the grade date has a space between the date and the time, and that 
it's only accurate to the minute.
The space will cause us some trouble in \cref{LastNameProblem}, and the minute 
accuracy means that two students can finish at exactly the same time 
(\cref{HandleTies}).

It's from this data that we want to compute the top list above,
the Top 10 \emph{on finishing fast}---this top list doesn't say anything about 
the level of mastery.

\ltnote{%
  \Cref{HighLevelApproach} and the following exercises will do two things.
  First, it will improve retention (\cite{Szekely1950}) by making the student 
  think about the problem first.
  Second, but related to the first, from a variation theoretic perspective 
  (\cite{NecessaryConditionsOfLearning}), it will allow the students to explore 
  a few alternatives on their own, and then get the necessary contrast to 
  better learn from the solution.
}%
\begin{exercise}[High-level approach]\label{HighLevelApproach}
  Outline a series of high-level steps to turn the input data into the desired 
  top 10 output above.
\end{exercise}

We'll do this in steps, more exactly in a series of pipes.
But it all starts with printing the data and passing it into this series of 
pipes.
We'll pass the CSV file containing all submission data as the first argument to 
the script (which we get through [[${1}]]).
The second argument ([[${2}]]) will be how many should be included in the top 
list.
This gives us the following (and one possible solution to 
\cref{HighLevelApproach}):
<<[[top.sh]]>>=
cat ${1} \
| <<filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed>> \
| <<sort the submission lines based on finishing time>> \
| <<keep only the last submission time for each student>> \
| head -n ${2}
@

\begin{pycode}
cmd = f"cat {submissions_file}"
\end{pycode}

The first two parts,
[[<<filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed>>]] and
[[<<sort the submission lines based on finishing time>>]],
are part of the base for the challenge.
These parts are covered in \cref{WhoPassed,SortingOnTime}.
The last part,
[[<<keep only the last submission time for each student>>]],
is part of the challenge.
We'll discuss the challenge and its solution\footnote{%
  We'll discuss both the solution provided by Skye Kaijser,
  and alternative ways of solving it.
} in \cref{ChallengeCase}.

\section{Filtering out everyone who has passed}\label{WhoPassed}

\begin{exercise}[Who passed?]
  How can we filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed all 
  assignments?
\end{exercise}

To solve the first task, that is
[[<<filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed>>]],
we must first find out the names of those who passed all assignments, and 
ignore everyone who hasn't passed the assignments.
Once we have those, we can keep only the lines containing one of those names.
<<filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed>>=
egrep "$(<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>)"
@ What the above means is simply that we'll run some commands from which we'll 
use the output as a regular expression to the above call to [[egrep]].
Then [[egrep]] will filter out only those whose name is in the regex.

Now, we need to filter out everyone who has finished all four assignments and 
then put their names into one long regex.
Something like this:
[[(Skye Kaijser|Daniel Bosk|Ric Glassey)]].

If we look at the example output from above, we see that there is a P in one of 
the columns on all assignments.
So we can reduce the whole thing to one line per student, containing name and 
the four grades.
For example: [[Axel Bergengren P P P P]] from the data above.
That way we can just check for lines with four P's.

\begin{exercise}
  Try to outline the steps that are needed to merge the four lines for each 
  student into one.
  That is turn
\begin{pycode}
shell(f"cat {submissions_file} | egrep '{examples}'")
\end{pycode}
  into
\begin{pycode}
shell(f"cat {submissions_file} | egrep '{examples}' \\" +
r"""
| cut -f 3,4 | sort \
| sed -nEz 's/([^\t\n]+)\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)/\1\t\2 \3 \4 \5/gp'
""")
\end{pycode}
\end{exercise}

To merge all lines with the same assignments, they must be sorted.
We want to sort by name of the student.
The only columns we need are the name of the student (column 3) and the grade 
(column 4), so we cut those.
Then we can sort, we sort on the name which is now the first column in this 
data.
<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>=
cat ${1} | cut -f 3,4 | sort \
@

\ltnote{%
  We need \cref{WhyNewCat} to bring to the students attention that this is a 
  sub-shell and that each sub-shell has its own stdin and stdout, and, 
  consequently, it's own series of pipes.

  From a variation theory perspective,
  they should have discered that each program has its own stdin and stdout and 
  that a pipe connects one program's stdout to the next program's stdin.
  But now we open up that we can read data from somewhere other than stdin to 
  inject new data into the pipe.
  We don't need to only use the data from the beginning.

  This should open up a new dimension of variation in the phenomenon of pipes.
}%
\begin{exercise}\label{WhyNewCat}
  Why do we need [[cat ${1}]] in
  [[<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>]]?
\end{exercise}

We need to use [[cat ${1}]] to get the data from the file.
Although this data is already in the pipe from the start of
[[<<[[top.sh]]>>]],
that data is piped into stdin of [[egrep]] in
[[<<filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed>>]].
That's why we need to read the file again here, to create another series of 
pipes.
We can see in
[[<<filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed>>]]
that
[[<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>]]
is run in a sub-shell, since it's enclosed in parenthesis ([[$(...)]]).

By now, the data in the pipe will look like this:
\begin{pycode}
regex_cmd = f"cat {submissions_file}" \
            f"| cut -f 3,4 | sort"
shell(regex_cmd + f" | egrep '{examples}'")
\end{pycode}

Now we want to merge all lines matching the same student into one line,
[[Axel Bergengren P P P P]].
We'll use [[sed]].
This is pattern matching over several lines and [[sed]] normally does matching 
over single lines.
The [[-z]] option allows us to match over several lines.
The [[-E]] option says we want to use extended regexes\footnote{%
  Which is the syntax you'll learn in later courses.
  That extended syntax also allows us to use less escaping when writing the 
  regex.
}.
Finally, the [[-n]] option says we shouldn't print any lines that doesn't 
match.
(That's why we need the [[p]] at the end, to print the result.)
The [[g]] close to the end says we want to do global matching, not just the 
first match.
<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>=
| sed -nEz 's/<<name and grades to match>>/<<all on one line pattern>>/gp' \
@

\begin{exercise}
  What should we try to match in our regex?
  What must we think about to get those matches right?
  How can we use the matches to get the end result that we want?
\end{exercise}

We'll match a name ([[[^\t\n]+]]) followed by a grade ([[[PF]]]), followed by 
a new line ([[\n]]), followed by the same name ([[\1]]), another grade (not the 
same as the first, hence no back reference, [[\2]]).
These are separated by tabs in the data, but we use [[\s]] that matches any 
whitespace character (easier to write).

The name is simply everything up to the tab: [[[^\t\n]]] matches any character 
that is neither a tab nor a new line, and the [[+]] says that we want at least 
one such character, but as many as possible\footnote{%
  We can't use [[.]] here, the usual \enquote{any character}.
  The [[-z]] option makes [[sed]] read the whole file as one long line, so the 
  new lines are ordinary characters that [[.]] would happily match.
}.
\ltnote{%
  \Cref{WhyNotLetters} contrasts the robust pattern with the tempting one that 
  the first version of this text actually used.
  The critical aspect to discern is that a regex only matches what we tell it 
  to; whatever we forget to include silently disappears.
  Seeing a real student vanish from the top list makes that consequence 
  concrete.
}%
\begin{exercise}\label{WhyNotLetters}
  Why don't we simply list the letters that a name can contain, like 
  [[[A-Za-z -]+]]?
  What happens to a student whose name doesn't match?
\end{exercise}
The first version of this text did exactly that.
But names contain more than the English alphabet: almost a fifth of this 
year's students have an å, ä, ö or é in their name.
\enquote{Wilmer Schéele} never matched, so Wilmer's four lines were never 
merged into one line with four P's, and Wilmer simply disappeared from the top 
list---without any error message.
It's safer to say what a name is \emph{not}: it can't contain the tab that ends 
it, nor a new line.
<<name and grades to match>>=
([^\t\n]+)\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)
<<all on one line pattern>>=
\1\t\2 \3 \4 \5
@ We use references back to the name and grades.
We want a tab character ([[\t]]) separating the name from the grades.
With that tab character, we can easily use [[cut]] to take the name only when 
we need it later.
The output so far is the following, as desired:
\begin{pycode}
regex_cmd += r'| sed -nEz "' \
             r's/([^\t\n]+)\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)\n\1\s([PF]?)' \
             r'/\1\t\2 \3 \4 \5/gp"'
shell(regex_cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}'")
\end{pycode}

Now we want to filter out only those who have four P's.
And we only want to keep the names, the first column (thanks to that tab we 
used above).
<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>=
| grep 'P P P P' | cut -f 1 \
@

The data in the pipe now looks like this:
\begin{pycode}
regex_cmd += r'| grep "P P P P" | cut -f 1'
shell(regex_cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}'")
\end{pycode}

\begin{exercise}
  How can we turn a list of names into the regex that we want?
  That is:
\begin{pycode}
shell(regex_cmd + rf"""\
| egrep '{examples}' \
| (echo -n '('; tr '\n' '|' | sed 's/|$//'; echo -n ')')
""")
\end{pycode}
\end{exercise}

At this point, the data coming out of the pipe is a list of names: one name per 
line.
We want to change from one name per line to the desired regex:
[[(Skye Kaijser|Daniel Bosk|Ric Glassey)]].
This means we start with an opening parenthesis ([[(]]),
translate each new line ([[\n]]) to a pipe ([[|]], a regex disjunction).
The last one will get an extra pipe that we don't want,
so we remove it using [[sed]].
<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>=
| (echo -n '('; tr '\n' '|' | sed 's/|$//'; echo -n ')')
@ But why do we enclose these two statements in parenthesis?
That creates a sub-shell, so that we can use [[echo]] without including it in 
the pipe.
It's only [[tr]] that takes the piped data as input, and in turn pipes it to 
[[sed]], which finally pipes it out.
After that we have the last [[echo]] that adds the closing parenthesis.

Why do we want to use that extra [[echo]] instead of just making the [[sed]] 
command add it?
Well, the [[sed]] will not add the closing parenthesis when the list is empty.
When the list is empty, there will not be any [[|]] by the end to replace, but 
we still need to close it to make it a syntactically valid expression.

When we use our example from above, we get:
\begin{pycode}
make_regex = r'| (echo -n "("; tr "\n" "|" | sed "s/|$//"; echo -n ")")'
shell(regex_cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}'" + make_regex)
regex_cmd += make_regex
\end{pycode}

That concludes
[[<<filter out the submission lines of everyone who passed>>]].
We now get the following.
\begin{pycode}
cmd += f' | egrep "$({regex_cmd})"'
shell(cmd + f" | egrep '{examples}' | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}
This is in fact the same data as when we started above.
Everyone in the example data had passed.
That's because I picked the examples from the top list.
I didn't want to include someone who had not passed, that might be a bit 
sensitive.
But rest assured that only those who have passed will be included in the data 
in the pipe at this point.

\section{Sorting the submission lines based on finishing 
time}\label{SortingOnTime}

Now we should proceed to
[[<<sort the submission lines based on finishing time>>]].
The data that we have in the pipe now are the lines one everyone who has 
passed.

We simply want to cut out the columns we need and then sort them based on 
the time stamp.
The columns we need are the name (column 3) and the grade date (column 6).
Remember that the submission date (column 5) is always [[N/A]] for these 
assignments, it's the grade date that says when a student passed.
\begin{pycode}
cmd += " | cut -f 3,6 | sort -k 2 -t '\t'"
shell(cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}' | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}

\begin{exercise}
  How can we cut out the columns that we need and then sort on the time stamp?
  (You should be able to come up with the exact commands and options, perhaps 
  with the help of the manual pages.
  Try to not peek at the solution below.)
\end{exercise}

We do this as follows.
<<sort the submission lines based on finishing time>>=
cut -f 3,6 | sort -k 2 -t $'\t'
@


\section{The challenge, or, creating the top list}\label{ChallengeCase}

Let's turn to the challenge and its solution.
%
\begin{pycode}
subprocess.run(f"""
mv times.txt times-full.txt
grep -E '{examples}' times-full.txt > times.txt
""", shell=True)
\end{pycode}
%
\begin{exercise}[The challenge]
  How can we take the output just above, that is
\begin{pycode}
shell(cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}' | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}
  where every name occurs four times and with different time stamps each time.
  We want to filter it so that every name occurs only once and with the latest 
  time stamp.
  We then want to number those lines to get the top list to get this:
\begin{pycode}
shell("bash skye.sh")
\end{pycode}
\end{exercise}

\subsection{Skye's solution}

Let's first have a look at Skye's solution.
I have added some indentation to make the different parts easier to discern.
I also added the [[head]] command at the end to only print the top 10 (which 
wasn't a part of the given challenge).
Finally, I replaced Skye's [[sort +2]] with [[sort -k 3]], which means the 
same thing\footnote{%
  The [[+2]] is an old way of writing it, where the columns are counted from 
  zero, so [[+2]] means \enquote{start at the third column}.
  The [[sort]] on today's computers no longer accepts it.
}.
<<[[skye.sh]]>>=
sort times.txt \
| awk '{print $1,$2}' \
| uniq \
| while read p; do \
    cat times.txt \
    | grep -E "$p" \
    | tail -1; \
  done \
| sort -k 3 \
| while read line; do \
    num=$((num+1)); \
    echo "$num $line"; \
  done \
| head -n 10
@

\begin{exercise}
  Try to explain what Skye's code does and why it solves the problem.
\end{exercise}

First it sorts on name ([[sort times.txt]]), since the name is the first 
column.
Then [[awk]] gets the name, but only the first two names---not if a person has 
more than two names.
(This should work in most cases, unless we have \enquote{Anders Anderson} and 
\enquote{Anders Anderson Bertilsson}, then both will be included.)
We could have used [[cut -f 1]] (remember that tab character separating them) 
instead of that line of [[awk]].
Then [[uniq]] removes duplicates.

The first [[while]] loop reads these names from the pipe.
It then reads the times from the file, it filters out a person's times and 
keeps the last one ([[tail -1]]).
Remember, this data is sorted on times, so it's the correct one.

The output from the first [[while]] loop contains the correct lines, but 
they're no longer in the correct order.
(Since we [[grep]]ed on name.)
Thus we need [[sort -k 3]] to sort on time again.

The last [[while]] loop numbers the lines.

Skye wrote the code to read the input from a file, [[times.txt]].
We can create that one by using the [[tee]] command in the series of pipes.
The command [[tee]] writes the data to a file and then passes it on in the 
pipes.
It writes the same thing to a file and to stdout, which is stdin for the next 
command in the line of pipes.
<<keep only the last submission time for each student>>=
tee times.txt \
@

If we run Skye's code on the output from the pipe above, that is the output 
from [[<<sort the submission lines based on finishing time>>]], which is stored 
in [[times.txt]], we get the following.
\begin{pycode}
shell("bash skye.sh")
\end{pycode}

\ltnote{%
  \Cref{LastNameProblem} should make the student think about the problem with 
  names that can be several words.
  We want them to discern that more words changes the number of columns if we 
  are not careful.
}
\begin{exercise}\label{LastNameProblem}
  Why is Elias in third place instead of first in the output above?
\end{exercise}

Since Elias has one more word in his name, the [[sort -k 3]] in Skye's 
solution doesn't work as expected:
[[sort -k 3]] sorts on the third \emph{white-space delimited} column and 
onwards.
For Axel and Wilmer, the third column is the date, followed by the time.
(Remember that the time stamp contains a space, so it's two columns by 
itself.)
For Elias, however, the third column is Fourati, and letters sort after 
digits.

To fix it, we can change the [[sort -k 3]] to [[sort -k 2 -t $'\t']], to 
separate columns on tabs instead of white space in general (space, tabs, 
etc.).
Note that we must change the column number too: with tabs as the only 
separator, the name is the first column and the whole time stamp is the 
second.

This illustrates how easy it is to introduce a bug.
I also had this bug in the original version of my script.
So the bug in my script caused the input data that Skye had to work with to 
only have two-word names.
Consequently, Skye's solution worked without error for that input data.

\subsection{An alternative solution}

We can solve this in a different way.
Actually, we can solve it in many different ways, but I'll only give one 
alternative solution.

As mentioned above, we should read it from below.
We should only keep the last line for each person.
So, we just sort it in the other order.
<<keep only the last submission time for each student>>=
| sort -k 2 -r -t $'\t' \
@

This yields the following in the pipe.
\begin{pycode}
cmd += " | sort -k 2 -r -t '\t'"
shell(cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}' | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}

Now we can read it in order, line by line.
Then we can ignore any future lines for the same name.
Note that we must keep quotation marks around [[line]] whenever we print it 
using [[echo]], otherwise we'll lose the tabs.
<<keep only the last submission time for each student>>=
| (names=$(mktemp); \
   while read line; do \
     name=$(echo "$line" | cut -f 1); \
     <<if [[name]] is in [[names]], take next line; otherwise continue below>> \
     echo "$line"; \
     echo $name >> $names; \
   done) \
@

There are two interesting keywords that can be used in a loop:
[[break]] and [[continue]].
The [[break]] keyword will break out of the loop, that is terminate it 
prematurely; while [[continue]] will skip the rest of the loop body and 
continue with the next iteration.
The [[continue]] keyword seems useful to us, we can do this:
If we can find the name in the file, we skip to the next using [[continue]].
<<if [[name]] is in [[names]], take next line; otherwise continue below>>=
grep -q "$name" $names && continue;
@ So what does the [[&&]] do?
It's a logical and.
It will evaluate to true if both programs exit with success (not an error, 
meaning they will return 0).
It's evaluated a bit lazily, if the first program fails, there is no chance of 
the expression turning true, so it will not execute the next command.
This means that [[continue]] will only be executed if the [[grep]] command 
succeeds, that is, if it finds the name in the file.
Otherwise, it will not execute the [[continue]] command and instead run the two 
[[echo]] commands.

This gives us the following data coming out of the pipe at this time:
\begin{pycode}
cmd += " | (names=$(mktemp); " \
       "while read line; do " \
       "name=$(echo \"$line\" | cut -f 1); " \
       "grep -q \"$name\" $names && continue; " \
       "echo \"$line\"; " \
       "echo $name >> $names; " \
       "done)"
shell(cmd + f" | egrep '{examples}' | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}

Now we must sort this on time again to get it in the correct order.
<<keep only the last submission time for each student>>=
| sort -k 2 -t $'\t' \
@ This turns the data in the pipe into:
\begin{pycode}
cmd += " | sort -k 2 -t '\t'"
shell(cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}' | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}

Finally, we must add the numbering.
We start [[num]] at zero, so that we can add one to it before printing it and 
the line.
<<keep only the last submission time for each student>>=
| (num=0; while read line; do num=$((num+1)); echo "$num $line"; done)
@ This gives us the final output:
\begin{pycode}
cmd_number = " | (num=0; while read line; do " \
             "num=$((num+1)); echo \"$num $line\"; done)"
shell(cmd + f"| egrep '{examples}'"
      + cmd_number + " | sed 's/\t/  /g'")
\end{pycode}


\section{Conclusion}

The final script is the following.
The base code is above and the challenge code below the highlighted line.
\inputminted[numbers=left,highlightlines=7]{bash}{top.sh}

If we run it on the full data, we get the same top list as in \cref{Overview}.
But the script doesn't care which year's data we give it.
It only relies on the columns being in the same places and on the time stamps 
sorting correctly as text, which they do since they start with the year, then 
the month, and so on.

\subsection{The top lists over the years}\label{OverTheYears}

\ltnote{%
  Running the same script on three years of data, one of them in an older 
  format, is the fusion step: the students see the pipeline as a tool that 
  generalises, and see that it generalises because it depends on nothing but 
  the column positions.
}%
So let's run it on the data from every year that the course has run in this 
form.
In 2024 and 2025, the LAB1 assignments were shared with the course DD1337 
(progd), so those lists contain the students of both courses.
We get that data with the same line as above, but with a regex that matches 
both courses, for instance [[-c "^(datintro|progd)25$"]].
The 2024 data is from an older version of [[canvaslms]], which wrote the time 
stamps differently (and wrote nothing instead of [[N/A]]).
As we said, the script doesn't mind.
\begin{pycode}
for year in (24, 25, 26):
  print(rf"\paragraph{{20{year}}}")
  shell(f"bash ./top.sh submissions{year}.csv 10")
\end{pycode}

The only thing left to wish is that we could merge some of these into shared 
places.
In 2026, Jacob and Wilmer have exactly the same time stamp, since the time 
stamps are only accurate to the minute; it's only the order in which they were 
graded that puts Jacob before Wilmer.
In 2024, Oscar and Oskar were graded two seconds apart, they were in the same 
grading round, so they too finished at the same time for our purposes.

\begin{exercise}[Handle ties]\label{HandleTies}
  Write a script that takes lines with sufficiently close times and merges them 
  into one.
\end{exercise}

\subsection{Eric's solution}\label{EricsSolution}

\ltnote{%
  Eric's contribution gets the same treatment as our own script: first the 
  whole, as a run in the terminal, then the parts by stepwise refinement, 
  in the same order as the steps of [[<<[[top.sh]]>>]], so that the 
  student can compare the two solutions step by step.
  The exercises come before the explanations, as in the rest of the text.
  The script is presented as if written for this year's file.
  Eric's mail read the time from column 5, matched names with the letter 
  list of an earlier edition, and split the time stamps on the [[T]] of 
  the 2024 export; those lines were updated silently, since a reader 
  today has no old listing to compare with.
}%
Eric Gesien Andersson solved \cref{HandleTies} and sent me the script 
[[<<[[eric.sh]]>>]].
It doesn't extend [[<<[[top.sh]]>>]], it replaces it: it reads the CSV 
file itself, does the filtering and sorting in its own way, and then 
numbers the students with shared places.
This is how we run it on this year's data:
\begin{minted}{bash}
bash eric.sh submissions26.csv
\end{minted}
This gives us everyone who has passed so far, in finishing order, with 
shared places:
\begin{pycode}
shell("bash ./eric.sh submissions26.csv")
\end{pycode}
Compare this with the top list for 2026 above: Jacob and Wilmer now share 
seventh place, just as we wished for, and there is no eighth.

The script takes three options.
With [[-n]] we say how many lines we want, so [[-n 10]] gives us a top 
ten; without it we get up to 100 lines.
With [[-d]] we say how many seconds apart two students may be and still 
share a place; without it, 60 seconds.
Note that [[-n]] counts lines, not places: if we ask for seven lines we 
get eight, since the script would rather keep a shared place whole.
The third option, [[-f]], forces exactly the number of lines we asked 
for; with [[-n 7 -f]] Wilmer falls off.

\begin{exercise}\label{EricsSteps}
  Before we look at the script: what steps must it take to get from the 
  CSV file to the list above?
  Which of them does [[<<[[top.sh]]>>]] take too, and which are new?
\end{exercise}

Eric's script takes four steps.
The first three we recognise from [[<<[[top.sh]]>>]]: read what we asked 
for, find everyone who passed, find the latest time for each student.
The fourth is the numbering, now with shared places.
<<[[eric.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
<<[[eric.sh]] read the options>>
<<[[eric.sh]] find everyone who passed>>
<<[[eric.sh]] find the latest time for each student>>
<<[[eric.sh]] number the students, sharing places>>
@ We'll take the steps one at a time and refine each of them into smaller 
pieces, just like we did with [[<<[[top.sh]]>>]].

\subsubsection{Reading the options}

So far we have taken arguments by position: [[${1}]] is the file and 
[[${2}]] how many lines we want.
That works when there are exactly two and we always give both.
Eric has three optional settings, in any order, so he needs something 
better: first the default values, then a loop that reads the options one 
at a time, and finally [[shift]], which throws the options away so that 
the file name ends up in [[$1]] like before.
<<[[eric.sh]] read the options>>=
<<[[eric.sh]] default values>>
while getopts "fn:d:" opt; do
  <<[[eric.sh]] remember what the option says>>
done;
shift $((OPTIND -1));
@

The defaults are what we get when we give no options at all: 100 lines 
and 60 seconds.
The tab is written [[$'\t']] like in 
[[<<sort the submission lines based on finishing time>>]].
This is a [[bash]] script, not a script for just any shell: [[$'\t']] and 
the [[[[ ... ]]]] test below are [[bash]]'s own.
<<[[eric.sh]] default values>>=
delim=$'\t'
force=false
maxEntries=100
minTimeDifference=60
@

The string [[fn:d:]] lists the letters that we accept; a colon after a 
letter means that it takes a value, and [[getopts]] puts that value in 
[[OPTARG]].
A value for [[-n]] or [[-d]] must be a number, which Eric checks with 
[[bash]]'s own test [[[[ ... ]]]] and [[=~]] for regex matching: the 
[[^]] and [[$]] say that the whole value must be digits, just like in 
\cref{Overview}.
(A bad value is reported, and then the script keeps its default.)
When the loop is done, [[OPTIND]] points at the first argument that isn't 
an option, and that is what the [[shift]] above uses.
<<[[eric.sh]] remember what the option says>>=
if [ "$opt" == "f" ]; then
  force=true;
elif [ "$opt" == "n" ]; then
  if [[ "$OPTARG" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    maxEntries=$OPTARG;
  else
    echo "Integer required for -n";
  fi;
elif [ "$opt" == "d" ]; then
  if [[ "$OPTARG" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    minTimeDifference=$OPTARG;
  else
    echo "Integer required for -d";
  fi;
fi;
@

\subsubsection{Finding everyone who passed}

Who passed?
This is the same job as 
[[<<create pattern matching everyone who passed>>]], and Eric solves it 
the same way, only he keeps the result in a variable instead of piping it 
straight into [[egrep]].
<<[[eric.sh]] find everyone who passed>>=
peopleDone=$(cat "$1" | cut -d "$delim" -f 3,4 | sort -t "$delim" -k1 |
  <<[[eric.sh]] merge each student's lines into one>> |
  grep 'P P P P' | cut -d "$delim" -f 1);
peoplePattern="($(echo "$peopleDone" |
  <<[[eric.sh]] join the names into one regex>>))";
@

To merge the four lines of a student into one, Eric uses the very same 
[[sed]] as we do, so we simply reuse our chunks.
<<[[eric.sh]] merge each student's lines into one>>=
sed -n -E -z 's/<<name and grades to match>>/<<all on one line pattern>>/gp'
@

The names come out one per line, and Eric wants them joined with [[|]] 
into one regex, like we did.
We used [[tr]] to turn every new line into a [[|]] and then had to remove 
the last one.
Eric uses a small [[sed]] program instead: [[:a]] is a label, [[N]] appends 
the next line to the one that [[sed]] is holding, [[s/\n/|/]] replaces the 
new line that now sits between them, and [[ta]] jumps back to [[a]] if that 
replacement happened.
When there is no next line, [[N]] ends the loop.
So Eric only ever replaces a new line that has a name on both sides, and 
there is no trailing [[|]] to clean up.
(It's clever, but the [[tr]] version is easier to get right yourself.)
<<[[eric.sh]] join the names into one regex>>=
sed ':a; N; s/\n/|/; ta'
@

\subsubsection{Finding the latest time for each student}

Then Eric wants the latest time for each student, what we did in 
[[<<keep only the last submission time for each student>>]].
He takes the name and the grade date, columns 3 and 6, but first he 
removes something that we haven't seen yet.
[[canvaslms]] writes its output with Python's [[csv]] module, which ends 
every line with a carriage return before the new line, as the CSV 
standard says.
No listing shows it, but [[cat -A]] does, as [[^M]] (and the tabs as 
[[^I]]):
\begin{pycode}
shell(f"egrep '{examples}' {submissions_file} | head -n 1 | cat -A")
\end{pycode}
It survives [[cut]] and sticks to whatever is in the last column, here the 
time stamp, where it would make a mess of the arithmetic below.
So [[tr -d '\r']] removes it before it can do any harm.
The 2024 time stamps are then rewritten to look like today's, so that the 
rest of the script only needs to know one form.
<<[[eric.sh]] find the latest time for each student>>=
peopleTimes=$(cat "$1" | tr -d '\r' | grep -E "$peoplePattern" |
  cut -d "$delim" -f 3,6 |
  <<[[eric.sh]] write 2024's time stamps like today's>> |
  <<[[eric.sh]] keep only the latest line of each student>> |
  sort -t "$delim" -k2);
@

We saw in \cref{OverTheYears} that the 2024 export wrote the time stamps 
with a [[T]] and a [[Z]]: [[2024-08-26T09:32:39Z]].
This [[sed]] rewrites them into today's form.
It only touches a [[T]] that is followed by a time and a [[Z]] at the very 
end of the line, so the names are safe (think of Theo).
<<[[eric.sh]] write 2024's time stamps like today's>>=
sed -E 's/T([0-9:]+)Z$/ \1/'
@

Keeping only the latest line is the nicest trick in the script.
Eric sorts on the name and then on the time in \emph{reverse} 
([[-k1,1 -k2r,2]]), so that each student's four lines come together with 
the latest first.
Then [[paste - - - -]] glues four lines into one: each [[-]] means 
\enquote{read one line from standard input}, and four of them is no 
coincidence.
Finally [[cut -f 1,2]] keeps the first name and the first, that is the 
latest, time.
<<[[eric.sh]] keep only the latest line of each student>>=
sort -t "$delim" -k1,1 -k2r,2 | paste - - - - | cut -d "$delim" -f 1,2
@ This only works because every student has exactly four lines, which is 
what [[-A LAB1]] bought us in \cref{Overview}.

\begin{exercise}\label{FiveLines}
  What would [[paste - - - -]] do if one student had five lines?
  Would Skye's [[tail -1]] or our [[continue]] loop have the same 
  problem?
\end{exercise}

A fifth line would shift everything after it by one: [[paste]] would glue 
the fifth line to the next student's first three, and from there on every 
\enquote{student} would be a mix of two.
Skye's [[tail -1]] and our loop don't care how many lines there are, they 
just take the last one.
So these are three ways of saying \enquote{the last one}, but only two of 
them survive a fifth line.

\subsubsection{Numbering the students, sharing places}

Finally the numbering.
Eric reads the students in finishing order, counting the lines in 
[[rank]].
A student who finished together with the previous one shares the previous 
one's place; otherwise the student gets a new place, which is simply the 
line number.
To decide \enquote{together}, the script must compare two time stamps, and 
since a time stamp is text and we can't subtract text, Eric first defines 
a function for that.
<<[[eric.sh]] number the students, sharing places>>=
<<[[eric.sh]] function to compute time difference>>
previousTime="";
previousDate="";
rank=0;
streak=1;
echo "$peopleTimes" |
  while read line; do
    rank=$((rank + 1));
    <<[[eric.sh]] split the time stamp into date and time>>
    if <<[[eric.sh]] finished together with the previous one>>; then
      <<[[eric.sh]] share the place>>
    else
      <<[[eric.sh]] start a new place>>
    fi;
    previousTime="$currentTime";
    previousDate="$currentDate";
  done;
@

The function turns a time like [[11:42]] into the number of seconds since 
midnight, for both of its arguments, and subtracts.
It answers by [[echo]]ing the number; whoever calls it catches the answer 
with [[$( ... )]], the same sub-shell as in \cref{WhyNewCat}.
Two details are worth a closer look.
The [[${currentSeconds:-0}]] means \enquote{the value, or 0 if there is 
none}: this year's time stamps have no seconds.
The [[10#]] says that the number is written in base 10: without it, 
[[bash]] reads a number with a leading zero as octal, and [[08]] and [[09]] 
aren't valid octal numbers, so the script would crash on any time stamp 
containing [[08]] or [[09]].
<<[[eric.sh]] function to compute time difference>>=
getTimeDifference(){
  currentHours=$(echo "$1" | cut -d ':' -f 1);
  currentMinutes=$(echo "$1" | cut -d ':' -f 2);
  currentSeconds=$(echo "$1" | cut -d ':' -f 3);
  currentSeconds="${currentSeconds:-0}";
  totalCurrentSeconds=$((10#$currentHours * 60 * 60 \
    + 10#$currentMinutes * 60 + 10#$currentSeconds));

  oldHours=$(echo "$2" | cut -d ':' -f 1);
  oldMinutes=$(echo "$2" | cut -d ':' -f 2);
  oldSeconds=$(echo "$2" | cut -d ':' -f 3);
  oldSeconds="${oldSeconds:-0}";
  totalOldSeconds=$((10#$oldHours * 60 * 60 \
    + 10#$oldMinutes * 60 + 10#$oldSeconds));
  echo $(($totalCurrentSeconds - $totalOldSeconds));
}
@

\ltnote{%
  \Cref{Midnight} follows try-first, tell-later: it names the symptom 
  only, so that the student discerns the problem and its fix themselves; 
  both are told in \cref{EricImproved}.
}%
\begin{exercise}[Midnight]\label{Midnight}
  Eric points out himself that the function breaks at midnight: a student 
  graded at 23:59 and one graded at 00:01 the next day are two minutes 
  apart, but the function says \(-1438\) minutes.
  What's the problem?
  How can we fix it?
\end{exercise}

We'll fix it in \cref{EricImproved}.

Each line has the name, a tab and the time stamp; the time stamp has the 
date, a space and the time.
<<[[eric.sh]] split the time stamp into date and time>>=
currentDate=$(echo "$line" | cut -d "$delim" -f 2 | cut -d ' ' -f 1);
currentTime=$(echo "$line" | cut -d "$delim" -f 2 | cut -d ' ' -f 2);
@

Two students finished together when they were graded on the same date and 
less than [[minTimeDifference]] seconds apart.
(Read Eric's test backwards: it says that the limit is greater than the 
difference.)
The [[&&]] means that the time difference is only computed when the dates 
are equal, so the function is never called on the first line, when 
[[previousTime]] is still empty.
<<[[eric.sh]] finished together with the previous one>>=
[ "$currentDate" == "$previousDate" ] &&
  [ $minTimeDifference -gt \
    $(getTimeDifference $currentTime $previousTime) ]
@

When they finished together, the student gets the same place as the 
previous one.
[[streak]] counts how many lines the current shared place has used so far, 
so [[rank - streak]] is the line where the shared place began, and 
[[streak]] grows so that the next line repeats it too.
The [[-f]] option only matters here: without it, a shared place is never 
cut in half, even if that means more than [[-n]] lines.
<<[[eric.sh]] share the place>>=
if [ $force = true ] && [ $rank -gt $maxEntries ]; then
  break;
fi;
echo "$((rank - streak)) $line";
streak=$((streak + 1));
@

Otherwise the place is the line number, and a new streak begins.
This is also where we stop after [[-n]] lines.
<<[[eric.sh]] start a new place>>=
if [ $rank -gt $maxEntries ]; then
  break;
fi;
echo "$rank $line";
streak=1;
@ Let's trace it on the 2025 data below: Gustav gets 1, Hjalmar 2, Mauritz 
gets \(3 - 1 = 2\), Rea gets \(4 - 2 = 2\) and Theo 5.
Just like in athletics, two silver medals mean that there is no bronze.

\subsubsection{The other years}

That's the whole script.
Let's run it on 2024 and 2025 too, as in \cref{OverTheYears}.
\begin{pycode}
for year in (24, 25):
  print(rf"\paragraph{{20{year}}}")
  shell(f"bash ./eric.sh -n 10 -d 60 submissions{year}.csv")
\end{pycode}
Oscar and Oskar share third place in 2024, as we wished for.

\begin{exercise}[Strictly closer]\label{StrictlyCloser}
  In 2025, Hjalmar and Mauritz share second place, graded in the same 
  minute.
  Gustav and Hjalmar are only a minute apart, but they don't share first 
  place.
  Why not?
\end{exercise}

Hjalmar and Mauritz were graded in the same minute, so their difference is 
zero.
Gustav and Hjalmar are exactly 60 seconds apart, and the test is 
\emph{strictly} less than 60: sixty is not less than sixty.

\begin{exercise}[Eric's chain]\label{EricsChain}
  Eric points out a problem himself: the script compares each line only 
  with the one before it.
  If we allow 700 seconds in 2025, we get this:
\begin{pycode}
shell("bash ./eric.sh -n 8 -d 700 submissions25.csv")
\end{pycode}
  Theo and Nils share fifth place, although they are twelve minutes apart.
  How did that happen, and what would the script have to remember to avoid 
  it?
  (To be fair to Eric, \cref{HandleTies} asked for \enquote{sufficiently 
  close} without saying close to what.)
\end{exercise}

Each line is compared with the one before it only: Valentin is a minute 
after Theo, Elias eleven minutes after Valentin, and Nils in the same 
minute as Elias, each less than 700 seconds.
To avoid the chain, the script would have to remember the time of the 
\emph{first} student in the shared place and compare with that, instead 
of with [[previousTime]].
We'll do that in \cref{EricImproved}.

\subsection{Improving Eric's solution}\label{EricImproved}

\ltnote{%
  This section closes the loop on \cref{Midnight,EricsChain}: the 
  problems that the student was asked to discern are now fixed, and the 
  fix reuses every chunk of [[<<[[eric.sh]]>>]] that was fine, so the 
  student sees exactly how little has to change.
}%
\Cref{Midnight,EricsChain} found two things to fix, and both live in the 
last step.
So [[<<[[eric-improved.sh]]>>]] takes the first three steps from 
[[<<[[eric.sh]]>>]] as they are, and only the numbering is new.
<<[[eric-improved.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
<<[[eric.sh]] read the options>>
<<[[eric.sh]] find everyone who passed>>
<<[[eric.sh]] find the latest time for each student>>
<<[[eric-improved.sh]] number the students, sharing places>>
@

\subsubsection{Seconds since 1970 instead of since midnight}

The problem in \cref{Midnight} is that the function counts seconds since 
midnight, so the count starts over every day.
Any fixed point in time will do instead, as long as it's the same for 
everyone: the first submission, say, or simply 1970.
That is what [[date]] gives us with [[date -d "$1" +%s]], if we give it 
the whole time stamp, date and time.
The whole function shrinks to one line.
<<[[eric-improved.sh]] function to compute time difference>>=
getTimeDifference(){
  echo $(($(date -d "$1" +%s) - $(date -d "$2" +%s)));
}
@ Let's try the function across midnight, 00:01 minus 23:59 the day 
before.
We put the function and one call to it in a small script of its own:
<<[[midnight.sh]]>>=
<<[[eric-improved.sh]] function to compute time difference>>
getTimeDifference "2026-09-02 00:01" "2026-09-01 23:59"
@ Running it gives us
\begin{pycode}
shell("bash ./midnight.sh")
\end{pycode}
seconds, two minutes, as it should be.

\subsubsection{Comparing with the first in the place}

The problem in \cref{EricsChain} is that every student is compared with 
the previous one, so a shared place can grow one small step at a time.
Instead, we remember the time stamp of the student who \emph{opened} the 
current shared place, in [[firstTime]], and compare everyone with that.
It's only updated when a new place starts.
The date check is gone, since the function now handles the dates itself; 
instead we check that there is a first time stamp at all, which there 
isn't on the first line.
The two pieces that print the lines are Eric's, unchanged.
<<[[eric-improved.sh]] number the students, sharing places>>=
<<[[eric-improved.sh]] function to compute time difference>>
firstTime="";
rank=0;
streak=1;
echo "$peopleTimes" |
  while read line; do
    rank=$((rank + 1));
    currentTime=$(echo "$line" | cut -d "$delim" -f 2);
    if <<[[eric-improved.sh]] together with the first in the place>>; then
      <<[[eric.sh]] share the place>>
    else
      <<[[eric.sh]] start a new place>>
      firstTime="$currentTime";
    fi;
  done;
<<[[eric-improved.sh]] together with the first in the place>>=
[ -n "$firstTime" ] &&
  [ $minTimeDifference -gt \
    $(getTimeDifference "$currentTime" "$firstTime") ]
@

Let's run it on the chain from \cref{EricsChain}:
\begin{pycode}
shell("bash ./eric-improved.sh -n 8 -d 700 submissions25.csv")
\end{pycode}
Theo, Valentin, Elias and Nils are now two places, not one.

\begin{exercise}
  Run both scripts on this year's data with [[-d 60]].
  Do the lists differ?
  Why, or why not?
\end{exercise}

They don't: no shared place this year has more than two students, so 
there is no chain for the fix to break.
In 2024 there is a difference, though: a grading round at two in the 
morning gave Eric's script a chain of thirteen students on place 35, 
which the fix splits into three places.

